image: /infographics/03-hn-50124642.jpg
---

Agent harness design dictates inference efficiency far more than model parameter tuning. When benchmarking real production agent workloads, client harnesses trigger uncached input rates varying from 1.6 percent to over 11 percent, drastically swinging prefill latency and GPU energy consumption.

The underlying mechanism is strict prefix matching in modern prompt key-value caches. In a deep agentic session exceeding 128,000 tokens, a single turn that mutates earlier conversation history invalidates the entire subsequent cache. That single re-read requires as much prefill compute as roughly 600 cache-hit turns at that same context depth. In production measurements, a full cache miss more than doubles the total energy consumed per request.

Every single observed cache break stemmed not from model limitations, but from harnesses retroactively modifying messages that had already been sent. Treating conversation logs as an immutable append-only ledger is the single easiest way to protect prefill latency.

Clean prefix discipline beats hardware upgrades every single time.

