hn_id: '50118853'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50118853
image: /infographics/07-hn-50118853.jpg
---

Go popularized Communicating Sequential Processes, but its channel primitives hit severe architectural limits when composing complex synchronization patterns. The core limitation in Go is that a channel send or receive is an executable statement tied to immediate execution inside a select block.

Concurrent ML solves this by turning communication operations into first-class values called events. Instead of executing an operation immediately, you construct a value that describes the communication, wrap it with transformations, and synchronize on it only when ready.

This shift allows engineers to build composable abstractions that Go cannot cleanly represent. You can create negative acknowledgments, dynamically combine multiple guarded events, and clean up spawned goroutines automatically on timeout without leaking state or writing nested select ladders.

Treating communication as data rather than syntax turns fragile concurrency plumbing into modular, reusable primitives.

