title: Structuring binary serialization for fixed and variable sized data
source: hn
url: https://blog.demofox.org/2026/10/15/binary-serialization-in-df_game_data/
date: '2026-10-16'
authors:
- Atrix256
why_read: Read this to understand practical architectural trade-offs when designing binary serialization formats that balance fixed and dynamic data.
hn_id: '50118694'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50118694
---

Serializing plain structs to disk is straightforward when sizes are fixed at compile time. You write an element count followed by raw bytes, then memory map the file and read it directly via pointer indexing.

The complexity begins when variable-sized arrays and strings enter the layout. If you prefix every dynamic record with its byte size, random access degenerates into an O(N) scan across every preceding element in the sequence.

The df_game_data architecture resolves this trade-off by separating fixed-size metadata headers from contiguous dynamic data blobs. Fixed headers retain predictable offset calculations, while embedded offset pointers target variable-length payloads stored in contiguous backing storage. This structure preserves zero-copy memory mapping without sacrificing arbitrary element indexing.

Efficient binary serialization is rarely about exotic compression, it is about designing memory layouts that keep data access direct.

