Modern GPU architectures such as NVIDIA Hopper and Blackwell have broken the traditional uniform data-parallel execution model. High-performance kernels now rely on warp specialization, where different warps take on dedicated asynchronous roles like memory loading through Tensor Memory Accelerators or executing tensor core operations.

Because these units operate asynchronously, warps must synchronize through transaction barriers and arrive-wait counters. This turns every kernel into an asynchronous concurrent program prone to subtle data races and deadlocks that standard runtime profiling easily misses. SentryWard addresses this by applying formal verification directly to kernel PTX instructions against a specific launch configuration.

The verifier mathematically proves barrier synchronization correctness, validates byte counters for asynchronous memory copies, and establishes data-race freedom across shared, global, and tensor memory. It can also verify functional equivalence against a reference PyTorch implementation.

Catching synchronization bugs mathematically before executing on costly clusters eliminates weeks of chasing non-deterministic schedule corruptions.

